# Optional for local development: the engine runs fully without any of these.
# A production server needs the first three, and fails in a specific way without
# each — see README, "What production needs".

# The address readers use, e.g. https://example.com. Checkout redirects and the
# links in alert mail are built from it, because behind a reverse proxy a request
# only knows the server's own address. Production refuses to run without it
# rather than guess; locally it falls back to the dev server's address.
KAUTILYA_ORIGIN=

# The SQLite file; defaults to ./kautilya.db. In production, an absolute path
# OUTSIDE the build: the standalone server runs from .next/standalone, which each
# build replaces, and there the file holds accounts as well as the corpus.
KAUTILYA_DB=

# Protects GET /api/cron, the scheduled refresh.
CRON_SECRET=

# Deeper Chinese translation, framing analysis and narrative summaries.
# Absent, the deterministic engine handles everything. The only feature that
# spends money per click: it needs a signed-in reader, and the key's workspace
# should carry a spend limit.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Live Stripe billing; both keys are needed. Without them a development server
# runs checkout in test mode (Pro without payment) and production closes it.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Background corpus refresh. Off unless set: a default clone must not start
# fetching 73 live publisher feeds on its own. The interval floor is 15 minutes
# and is not overridable — every cycle hits those feeds for real. Use this OR a
# cron calling /api/cron, not both: nothing stops the two overlapping.
KAUTILYA_AUTO_INGEST=
KAUTILYA_INGEST_MINUTES=30

# Ladder alerts, delivered over SMTP. Without credentials nothing is sent and the
# ingest logs what it would have delivered, so the whole pipeline is exercisable
# without mailing anyone. Alerts are Desk Pro only and off until a reader opts in
# on /account.
#
# SMTP_USER is the full mailbox address, SMTP_PASS its password. Which host you
# want follows from where that address's mail is actually hosted — check its MX
# records rather than guessing from the domain name. Two worked examples:
#   Hostinger  smtp.hostinger.com:465, the mailbox password from hPanel
#   Gmail      smtp.gmail.com:465, and a 16-character App Password from
#              myaccount.google.com/apppasswords, which exists only once 2-Step
#              Verification is on — the ordinary password will not authenticate
# Port 465 is implicit TLS. Port 587 is STARTTLS and is required to upgrade, so a
# password is never sent in the clear either way. SMTP_HOST falls back to Gmail's
# if unset, which is only right if that is genuinely where your mail lives.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.hostinger.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
# Defaults to SMTP_USER. Most providers, Gmail included, rewrite From to whichever
# account authenticated rather than refusing a mismatch, so pointing this somewhere
# else mostly buys a header nobody will ever see.
ALERTS_FROM=
# Links in the mail use KAUTILYA_ORIGIN, at the top of this file.
